{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffRRei7YWtRXfMQPgpin","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The candidate passes the self-check, which prints `OK`. The short smoke fit gave an ELPD-LOO of −2530.9, well behind the current best model's −2317.6. That number comes from a single 100-draw chain, so it is only a rough guide. Novelty and convergence weren't checked; admission tests those.\n\n**Name:** `two_flip_memory_subjective_coin`\n\n**Hypothesis:** People read a sequence one flip at a time and remember only the last two flips. From those they predict the next flip, using their own belief about how a random coin behaves right after it switches and right after it repeats. A sequence looks random when its flips keep matching those expectations. So long streaks (a repeat after a repeat) and strict alternation (a switch after a switch after a switch) both look non-random. Each person picks the sequence their own imagined coin finds more likely, and has a small habitual lean toward clicking left or right.\n\n**Why this one:** the brief asked for a process account (memory, attention, step-by-step updating), and this is a working-memory limit plus sequential prediction. It targets three of the critique's discrepancies against the best model. Two survive the false-discovery correction: people choose the perfectly alternating sequence less often than predicted, and they prefer more variety of short patterns. The third, also surviving, is that the preference for switching levels off or reverses at high switch rates. A switch-then-switch expectation that differs from repeat-then-switch can produce all three, which a model predicting each flip from only the previous one cannot. That simpler model (`personal_markov_coin_belief`) was pruned, and this differs from `bayes_rigged_coin_with_two_flip_memory`, where the two-flip memory belongs to the rigged coin rather than to the person's picture of a random one.\n\n**Model:** each person has two beliefs: how likely the coin is to switch after a switch, and after a repeat. Both are hierarchical with spare slots for new participants. The model compares how probable each sequence is under that person's imagined coin, with one shared weight on that difference plus the person's left/right lean.\n\n**Admission risks:**\n- **Unreliable LOO estimate:** 6.7% of trials were above the Pareto-k limit, against a 1% tolerance. A full fit may bring that down.\n- **Possible slow fit:** the self-check alone took several minutes, and admission stops any sampling run after 30 minutes.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run2/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_3/candidate_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1155,"cache_read_input_tokens":71447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1155,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62104931-367f-460b-bf92-86f3ebe38ae3","uuid":"dc221390-0364-43f7-bebe-a473d840cf49","timestamp":"2026-10-03T13:58:29.186Z","request_id":"req_011CffRRdrn1CESDDVYSaRJX"}
